// stop_channel_start -- operation model §3.2, group 1. The non-blocking half
// of stop_channel(). Present on every profile.
//
// completes_when: STOP has been written. The abort itself has not necessarily
//                 happened yet.
// waits for:      nothing.
//
// An abort is reported as an ERROR, not as a clean stop -- the device sets ERR
// on a STOP write (§3.2.2). Carried into the contract verbatim.
//
// THIS FUNCTION CLAIMS NOTHING, and that is deliberate.
//
// By definition it aborts a transfer that is still running, so the channel it
// targets is already holding the `inflight` token. Claiming would report the
// one case the model explicitly permits as a violation. So stop is the
// documented exemption from the one-operation-per-channel rule, and now the
// exemption is visible in the code rather than only in a comment.
//
// TWO CONSEQUENCES A FIRMWARE CALLER MUST KNOW, both inherited unchanged:
//
//   * The read-modify-write below reads CHn_CSR, which clears ERR and the
//     interrupt sources. THIS IS STILL TRUE OF write_field: the LRM defines
//     the field-wise write as a read-modify-write (§21.14.1), so the read is
//     there even though the call no longer spells it out. That read happens
//     BEFORE STOP is written, so it can
//     only consume status that was already there -- the abort's own ERR is set
//     afterwards and is still seen by the aborted transfer's poll. What it can
//     lose is a completion that landed in the instant before the abort.
//
//   * WHO REPORTS THE ABORT IS NOT DETERMINED BY THIS MODEL. The aborted
//     transfer polls the same CSR, and whichever read lands first consumes the
//     ERR. On the non-blocking profile the loser's check_completion() answers
//     PENDING forever -- the same undetermined race the blocking layer has
//     always had, but a firmware caller sees it as a hang rather than as an
//     action that does not retire.
//
// There is no check_completion() pairing for stop on the non-blocking profile:
// stop holds no token, so it has nothing to poll with. A firmware caller
// aborts, and then keeps polling the TRANSFER it aborted -- which is the
// operation that owns the completion.
//
// open (§6.5): is write-1-to-STOP acknowledged before or after the current
//              WISHBONE cycle retires? That decides whether an abort can be
//              considered delivered on return, which matters post-silicon.
import wb_dma_regs_pkg::*;

extend component wb_dma_ch_c {

    target function void stop_channel_start() {
        // write_field IS the read-modify-write described above: §21.14.1
        // defines it as one. The CHn_CSR read, and everything the header says
        // about what that read consumes, is unchanged.
        regs.csr.write_field("stop", 1);
    }
}
